lessons.push({
  title: "Closures, Decorators with Arguments and functools Caching",
  minutes: 45,
  learn: ["Write decorators that take arguments", "Stack several decorators and know the order", "Use functools.lru_cache, cache, partial and cached_property", "Write a retry or timing decorator"],
  why: "Decorators implement cross-cutting concerns (logging, timing, retries, permissions, caching) once instead of inside every function. Caching turns slow repeated work into a dictionary lookup.",
  realWorld: "<code>@login_required</code>, <code>@app.route(\"/\")</code>, <code>@retry(times=3)</code>, <code>@lru_cache</code> in real codebases.",
  simple: "A decorator <em>with arguments</em> is a function that returns a decorator: <code>@repeat(3)</code> means <code>f = repeat(3)(f)</code>. Caching stores results by arguments so a repeated call is instant.",
  detail: `<h3>Decorator factories</h3>
<p>Three levels: the outer function takes the settings, returns the real decorator, which takes the function and returns the wrapper.</p>
<pre style="background:rgba(127,127,127,.14);padding:12px 14px;border-radius:8px"><code>def repeat(times):\n    def decorator(func):\n        @wraps(func)\n        def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n            for _ in range(times):\n                result = func(*args, **kwargs)\n            return result\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator</code></pre>
<h3>Stacking</h3>
<p>Decorators apply from the bottom up: <code>@a</code> above <code>@b</code> above <code>def f</code> means <code>f = a(b(f))</code>, so <code>a</code> is outermost and runs first on a call.</p>
<h3>functools</h3>
${table(["Tool", "Purpose"], [["<code>@cache</code>", "unlimited memoisation (3.9+)"], ["<code>@lru_cache(maxsize=128)</code>", "keeps only the most recent results; <code>.cache_info()</code>, <code>.cache_clear()</code>"], ["<code>partial(f, 2)</code>", "fix some arguments to make a simpler function"], ["<code>@cached_property</code>", "compute once per object, then store as an attribute"], ["<code>@wraps</code>", "keep name and docstring"], ["<code>@singledispatch</code>, <code>@total_ordering</code>", "see the OOP lessons"]])}
<p>Cached functions need <strong>hashable</strong> arguments (numbers, strings, tuples; not lists) and should be <strong>pure</strong> (same inputs always give the same output, no side effects). Caching an impure function returns stale answers.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `def retry(times):\n    def decorator(func):\n        ...\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\n@retry(times=3)\ndef fetch(): ...\nfrom functools import lru_cache, partial\ndouble = partial(pow, exp=2)`, note: "@repeat(3) calls repeat(3) first." },
  diagram: chain("A decorator factory", "Left to right: @repeat(3) calls repeat(3) which returns a decorator; the decorator wraps the function; the wrapper runs it three times.",
    [{ k: "proc", t: "repeat(3)" }, { k: "proc", t: "returns\ndecorator" }, { k: "proc", t: "decorator(f)\nreturns wrapper" }, { k: "proc", t: "wrapper runs\nf three times" }], { w: 135, gap: 28, hgt: 58 }),
  example: { code: `from functools import wraps, lru_cache, partial, cached_property\n\ndef repeat(times):\n    def decorator(func):\n        @wraps(func)\n        def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n            result = None\n            for _ in range(times):\n                result = func(*args, **kwargs)\n            return result\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\ncalls = []\n@repeat(3)\ndef hello(name):\n    calls.append(name)\n    return f"Hello, {name}"\nprint(hello("Asha"), len(calls))\n\ndef shout(func):\n    return lambda *a, **k: func(*a, **k).upper()\ndef exclaim(func):\n    return lambda *a, **k: func(*a, **k) + "!"\n@shout\n@exclaim\ndef greet():\n    return "hi"\nprint(greet())\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef slow_square(n):\n    return n * n\nslow_square(4); slow_square(4); slow_square(5)\ninfo = slow_square.cache_info()\nprint(info.hits, info.misses)\n\ndouble = partial(pow, exp=2)\nprint(double(7))\n\nclass Circle:\n    def __init__(self, r):\n        self.r = r\n    @cached_property\n    def area(self):\n        print("computing")\n        return 3 * self.r ** 2\nc = Circle(2)\nprint(c.area, c.area)`, out: `Hello, Asha 3\nHI!\n1 2\n49\ncomputing\n12 12` },
  lineByLine: [["def repeat(times)", "Receives the setting and returns the decorator."], ["@wraps(func)", "Keeps the original metadata."], ["@shout above @exclaim", "<code>shout(exclaim(greet))</code>: exclaim adds the mark, shout uppercases the result."], ["slow_square(4) twice", "The second call is a cache hit; cache_info shows 1 hit and 2 misses."], ["partial(pow, exp=2)", "A new function with <code>exp</code> already supplied."], ["@cached_property", "Runs once; the second access reuses the stored value, so 'computing' appears once."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "<code>@repeat(3)</code> on <code>hello</code>:", headers: ["Step", "What happens"], rows: [["definition", "repeat(3) returns decorator; decorator(hello) returns wrapper; the name hello now refers to wrapper"], ["hello(\"Asha\")", "wrapper calls the original 3 times"], ["result", "the last result is returned"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "A retry decorator that keeps trying a flaky function, with a counter to show it.", code: `from functools import wraps\n\ndef retry(times):\n    def decorator(func):\n        @wraps(func)\n        def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n            for attempt in range(1, times + 1):\n                try:\n                    return func(*args, **kwargs)\n                except ConnectionError:\n                    print("attempt", attempt, "failed")\n            raise ConnectionError("gave up")\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\nstate = {"left": 2}\n@retry(times=3)\ndef fetch():\n    if state["left"] > 0:\n        state["left"] -= 1\n        raise ConnectionError\n    return "data"\n\nprint(fetch())`, out: `attempt 1 failed\nattempt 2 failed\ndata` },
  mistakes: ["Forgetting the extra level: <code>@repeat</code> without brackets passes the function as <code>times</code>.", "Caching impure functions or functions with list arguments (TypeError: unhashable).", "Unbounded caches that grow forever on endless inputs.", "Misjudging decorator order.", "Swallowing exceptions in a retry decorator."],
  practices: ["Always <code>@wraps</code>.", "Use <code>lru_cache(maxsize=...)</code> for large input spaces.", "Keep decorators generic with <code>*args, **kwargs</code>."],
  interview: ["How do you write a decorator that accepts arguments?", "In what order do stacked decorators apply?", "What does lru_cache require of its arguments?", "What is functools.partial?"],
  practiceQs: ["Write a decorator that prints the function name and arguments.", "What does @a @b def f mean?", "Why doesn't lru_cache work with list arguments?", "Make a retry decorator that waits between attempts (idea only)."],
  revision: ["Decorator factory = three levels.", "Bottom decorator applies first.", "cache / lru_cache / cached_property / partial.", "Cache only pure functions."],
  hinglish: "Argument wala decorator teen level ka hota hai: bahar settings, beech mein decorator, andar wrapper. @cache aur @lru_cache purane results yaad rakhte hain. Sirf pure functions ko cache karo.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What does @repeat(3) mean?", ["f = repeat(f)(3)", "f = repeat(3)(f)", "f = repeat(3, f)", "repeat(3) runs f"], 1, "repeat(3) is evaluated first, and its result decorates f."),
    mcq("Which can be the argument of an lru_cache function?", ["a list", "a dict", "a tuple of numbers", "a set"], 2, "Arguments must be hashable."),
    mcq("For @a above @b above def f, which is the outermost wrapper?", ["a", "b", "f", "none"], 0, "f = a(b(f))."),
    mcq("What does partial(pow, exp=2)(7) return?", ["14", "49", "9", "Error"], 1, "pow(7, exp=2) = 49.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("Why use cached_property?", ["To compute an attribute once per object", "To make attributes private", "To speed up imports", "To create class variables"], 0, "The value is stored after the first access.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Count the ways to climb", "A staircase has n steps and you climb 1 or 2 steps at a time. Using a cached recursive function print the number of different ways to reach the top (n is between 1 and 80).", "from functools import cache\n\n@cache\ndef ways(n):\n    pass\n\nprint(ways(int(input())))\n", `from functools import cache\n\n@cache\ndef ways(n):\n    if n <= 2:\n        return n\n    return ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2)\n\nprint(ways(int(input())))`, [["5", "8"], ["1", "1"]], [["5", "8"], ["1", "1"], ["2", "2"], ["10", "89"], ["50", "20365011074"]])],
});

