lessons.push({
  title: "enumerate, zip, any, all, Unpacking, Walrus and Context Managers",
  minutes: 50,
  learn: ["Combine sequences with zip and number them with enumerate", "Use any, all, sum, min and max with generator expressions", "Unpack nested structures and use the walrus operator", "Write your own context manager"],
  why: "These small built-ins replace many index-based loops and make code shorter and safer. Context managers guarantee cleanup in every code path.",
  realWorld: "Pairing names with marks, checking that every field is filled, reading a line and testing it in one expression, locking a resource and always releasing it.",
  simple: "<code>zip</code> walks several sequences together, <code>enumerate</code> adds a counter, <code>any</code>/<code>all</code> answer &quot;is at least one / is every...&quot; questions, and a <strong>context manager</strong> runs setup and cleanup around a <code>with</code> block.",
  detail: `<h3>Built-ins</h3>
${table(["Tool", "Example", "Result"], [["<code>enumerate(seq, start=1)</code>", "<code>list(enumerate(\"ab\", 1))</code>", "[(1, 'a'), (2, 'b')]"], ["<code>zip(a, b)</code>", "<code>list(zip([1, 2], \"xy\"))</code>", "[(1, 'x'), (2, 'y')]; stops at the shortest (<code>strict=True</code> raises on unequal lengths, 3.10+)"], ["<code>zip(*rows)</code>", "transpose a matrix", "columns as tuples"], ["<code>dict(zip(keys, values))</code>", "build a dictionary", "{'a': 1, ...}"], ["<code>any(iterable)</code>", "<code>any(x &gt; 5 for x in nums)</code>", "True if at least one is true; <code>any([])</code> is False"], ["<code>all(iterable)</code>", "<code>all(x &gt; 0 for x in nums)</code>", "True if every one is true; <code>all([])</code> is True"], ["<code>reversed</code>, <code>sorted</code>", "<code>for x in reversed(seq)</code>", "iterate backwards, sort a copy"]])}
<h3>Unpacking</h3>
<p><code>a, (b, c), *rest = [1, (2, 3), 4, 5]</code>. In function calls: <code>f(*args, **kwargs)</code>. In literals: <code>[*a, *b]</code>, <code>{**d1, **d2}</code>. In loops: <code>for i, (name, mark) in enumerate(pairs):</code>.</p>
<h3>The walrus operator :=</h3>
<p><code>if (n := len(items)) &gt; 10:</code> assigns and tests in one expression (Python 3.8+). Handy in <code>while (line := f.readline()):</code>, but use sparingly: clarity first.</p>
<h3>Context managers</h3>
<p>Any object with <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code> works in <code>with</code>. The easy way is <code>contextlib.contextmanager</code>: write a generator with one <code>yield</code>; code before it is setup, code after is cleanup (in <code>try/finally</code>). <code>contextlib.suppress(KeyError)</code> ignores a specific error; <code>redirect_stdout</code> captures printed output.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `for i, (a, b) in enumerate(zip(xs, ys), start=1): ...\nany(x < 0 for x in nums)\nif (n := len(data)) > 3: ...\n@contextmanager\ndef timer():\n    start = ...\n    try:\n        yield\n    finally:\n        ...`, note: "zip stops at the shortest input." },
  diagram: chain("zip and enumerate together", "Left to right: names and marks go into zip, which produces pairs, enumerate numbers the pairs, and the loop unpacks index, name and mark.",
    [{ k: "io", t: "names\nmarks" }, { k: "proc", t: "zip(names, marks)" }, { k: "proc", t: "enumerate(..., 1)" }, { k: "io", t: "i, (name, mark)" }], { w: 150, gap: 28, hgt: 56 }),
  example: { code: `from contextlib import contextmanager, suppress\n\nnames = ["Asha", "Ravi", "Zoya"]\nmarks = [80, 92, 75]\nfor i, (name, mark) in enumerate(zip(names, marks), start=1):\n    print(i, name, mark)\nprint(dict(zip(names, marks)))\nprint(list(zip(*[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])))\nprint(any(m > 90 for m in marks), all(m >= 75 for m in marks), any([]), all([]))\nfirst, *middle, last = marks\nprint(first, middle, last)\nif (count := len(names)) > 2:\n    print("many names:", count)\n\n@contextmanager\ndef section(title):\n    print("start", title)\n    try:\n        yield\n    finally:\n        print("end", title)\n\nwith section("report"):\n    print("working")\n\nwith suppress(KeyError):\n    {}["missing"]\n    print("not reached")\nprint("continued")`, out: `1 Asha 80\n2 Ravi 92\n3 Zoya 75\n{'Asha': 80, 'Ravi': 92, 'Zoya': 75}\n[(1, 4), (2, 5), (3, 6)]\nTrue True False True\n80 [92] 75\nmany names: 3\nstart report\nworking\nend report\ncontinued` },
  lineByLine: [["enumerate(zip(names, marks), start=1)", "Numbers the pairs from 1; the loop header unpacks the nested structure."], ["zip(*[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])", "The star feeds the rows as separate arguments, which transposes the table."], ["any(...), all(...), any([]), all([])", "Empty input gives False for any and True for all."], ["first, *middle, last = marks", "Star collects the middle items."], ["(count := len(names)) > 2", "Assigns and tests together."], ["@contextmanager ... yield", "Code before yield is setup, after it is cleanup (finally)."], ["suppress(KeyError)", "The error is swallowed and the block ends early."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "How <code>zip(*[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])</code> works:", headers: ["Step", "Value"], rows: [["rows", "[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]"], ["*rows", "zip([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6])"], ["pairs taken in turn", "(1, 4), (2, 5), (3, 6)"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Validation with all/any and a clean walk through two lists at once.", code: `form = {"name": "Asha", "email": "a@x.in", "phone": ""}\nrequired = ["name", "email", "phone"]\nmissing = [f for f in required if not form[f]]\nprint("complete" if all(form[f] for f in required) else f"missing: {missing}")\nprices = [100, 250, 80]\nqtys = [2, 1, 5]\nprint(sum(p * q for p, q in zip(prices, qtys)))`, out: `missing: ['phone']\n850` },
  mistakes: ["Using <code>range(len(x))</code> when <code>enumerate</code> or <code>zip</code> is clearer.", "Forgetting that zip silently drops extra items.", "Iterating a zip twice (it is an iterator).", "Overusing the walrus operator.", "Forgetting <code>try/finally</code> in a generator-based context manager."],
  practices: ["Prefer <code>enumerate</code> to manual counters.", "Use <code>zip(..., strict=True)</code> when lengths must match.", "Keep generator expressions inside <code>any</code>/<code>all</code>/<code>sum</code> to avoid building lists."],
  interview: ["What does zip do with unequal lengths?", "What do any([]) and all([]) return?", "What is a context manager?", "What does the walrus operator do?"],
  practiceQs: ["Build a dictionary from two lists.", "Transpose [[1, 2], [3, 4]].", "Write a context manager that prints elapsed time.", "Check that all numbers in a list are positive."],
  revision: ["enumerate(start), zip, zip(*rows).", "any / all and the empty cases.", "first, *rest unpacking; := walrus.", "with + __enter__/__exit__ or @contextmanager."],
  hinglish: "zip kai sequences ko ek saath chalata hai, enumerate counter jodta hai. any aur all se 'koi ek' ya 'sab' wali jaanch hoti hai. Cleanup ke liye with aur context manager use karo.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What is list(zip([1, 2, 3], \"ab\"))?", ["[(1, 'a'), (2, 'b')]", "[(1, 'a'), (2, 'b'), (3, None)]", "Error", "[1, 2]"], 0, "zip stops at the shortest input.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("What is all([])?", ["True", "False", "None", "Error"], 0, "Vacuously true.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("What does enumerate(\"ab\", start=5) yield first?", ["(0, 'a')", "(5, 'a')", "('a', 5)", "(1, 'a')"], 1, "Counting starts at 5.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("What does := do?", ["Compares two values", "Assigns a value inside an expression", "Declares a type", "Creates a slice"], 1, "The walrus operator assigns within an expression."),
    mcq("In a @contextmanager function, where does the cleanup go?", ["Before yield", "After yield (usually in finally)", "In __init__", "Nowhere"], 1, "Code after the yield runs when the with block ends.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Pair names with marks", "Read a line of names and then a line of marks. Print each pair as name mark on its own line (use zip).", "names = input().split()\nmarks = input().split()\n# print name and mark pairs\n", `names = input().split()\nmarks = input().split()\nfor name, mark in zip(names, marks):\n    print(name, mark)`, [["Asha Ravi Zoya\n80 92 75", "Asha 80\nRavi 92\nZoya 75"], ["A\n1", "A 1"]], [["Asha Ravi Zoya\n80 92 75", "Asha 80\nRavi 92\nZoya 75"], ["A\n1", "A 1"], ["x y\n5 6", "x 5\ny 6"], ["p q r s\n1 2 3 4", "p 1\nq 2\nr 3\ns 4"]])],
});

