lessons.push({
  title: "Type Hints, Annotations and Structural Pattern Matching",
  minutes: 50,
  learn: ["Add type hints to variables, parameters and return values", "Use modern syntax: list[int], int | None, Callable, TypeVar, Protocol", "Understand that hints are not enforced at run time and how checkers use them", "Write match/case statements (Python 3.10+)"],
  why: "Type hints make large programs readable and let tools (editors, mypy, pyright) find bugs before the code runs. Pattern matching replaces long if-elif chains that inspect structure.",
  realWorld: "FastAPI builds validation and documentation from type hints; a command interpreter uses <code>match</code> on the shape of a command.",
  simple: "A hint is a label: <code>def total(prices: list[float]) -&gt; float</code>. Python <em>does not check it while running</em>; tools do. <code>match</code> compares a value against patterns and unpacks it at the same time.",
  detail: `<h3>Annotations</h3>
${table(["Hint", "Meaning"], [["<code>x: int = 5</code>", "variable annotation"], ["<code>def f(a: str, b: int = 0) -&gt; bool:</code>", "parameters and return"], ["<code>list[int]</code>, <code>dict[str, float]</code>, <code>tuple[int, str]</code>", "containers (built-in generics, 3.9+)"], ["<code>int | None</code>", "union (3.10+); older: <code>Optional[int]</code>, <code>Union[int, str]</code>"], ["<code>Callable[[int], str]</code>", "a function taking int and returning str"], ["<code>Any</code>", "opt out of checking"], ["<code>TypeVar</code>, generics", "functions that work for many types: <code>def first(items: list[T]) -&gt; T</code>"], ["<code>Protocol</code>", "structural interface: anything with the right methods matches"]])}
<p>Check with <code>mypy</code> or <code>pyright</code>; editors show the same checks. Annotations are stored in <code>__annotations__</code>. Since Python 3.14 they are evaluated lazily, so forward references need no quotes; on earlier versions use quotes (<code>"Node"</code>) or <code>from __future__ import annotations</code>.</p>
<h3>match / case</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Literal</strong> patterns: <code>case "quit":</code>; <strong>capture</strong>: <code>case ["move", x, y]:</code> binds x and y.</li><li><strong>OR</strong> with <code>|</code>, <strong>guards</strong> with <code>if</code>, wildcard <code>case _:</code>.</li><li><strong>Sequence</strong>, <strong>mapping</strong> (<code>case {"type": "circle", "r": r}</code>) and <strong>class</strong> patterns (<code>case Point(x=0, y=y)</code>).</li><li>Plain names in a pattern <em>capture</em>; to compare with a constant use a dotted name (<code>Color.RED</code>) or a literal.</li></ul>
<p>match needs Python 3.10 or newer. It is structural unpacking, not a C-style switch.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `def average(values: list[float]) -> float | None:\n    ...\nmatch command.split():\n    case ["move", x, y]:\n        ...\n    case ["quit"] | ["exit"]:\n        ...\n    case _:\n        ...`, note: "Hints are for people and tools, not for the interpreter." },
  diagram: graph("Hints are checked by tools, not by Python", "Source code with type hints goes to a checker like mypy which reports mistakes before running; Python itself runs the same code ignoring the hints.", 640, 180,
    [{ id: "s", k: "io", t: "code with hints", x: 100, y: 90, w: 160, h: 44 }, { id: "m", k: "proc", t: "mypy / editor\nfinds mistakes", x: 340, y: 45, w: 180, h: 54 }, { id: "p", k: "proc", t: "Python runs it\n(ignores hints)", x: 340, y: 135, w: 190, h: 54 }, { id: "r", k: "io", t: "report /\noutput", x: 560, y: 90, w: 130, h: 50 }],
    [{ from: "s", to: "m" }, { from: "s", to: "p" }, { from: "m", to: "r" }, { from: "p", to: "r" }]),
  example: { code: `from typing import Callable, Protocol, TypeVar\n\ndef average(values: list[float]) -> float | None:\n    return sum(values) / len(values) if values else None\n\nprint(average([1, 2, 3]), average([]))\nprint(average.__annotations__["return"])\n\nT = TypeVar("T")\ndef first(items: list[T]) -> T:\n    return items[0]\nprint(first(["a", "b"]), first([3, 4]))\n\ndef apply(func: Callable[[int], int], value: int) -> int:\n    return func(value)\nprint(apply(lambda n: n * 2, 21))\n\nclass Speaker(Protocol):\n    def speak(self) -> str: ...\nclass Dog:\n    def speak(self) -> str:\n        return "woof"\ndef talk(s: Speaker) -> str:\n    return s.speak()\nprint(talk(Dog()))\n\ndef run(command: str) -> str:\n    match command.split():\n        case ["move", x, y]:\n            return f"moving to ({x}, {y})"\n        case ["say", *words]:\n            return "saying: " + " ".join(words)\n        case ["quit"] | ["exit"]:\n            return "bye"\n        case [name, *_] if name.startswith("#"):\n            return "comment"\n        case _:\n            return "unknown"\nfor c in ["move 3 4", "say hello world", "exit", "# note", "jump"]:\n    print(run(c))\n\npoint = {"type": "circle", "r": 2}\nmatch point:\n    case {"type": "circle", "r": r}:\n        print("circle of radius", r)\n    case {"type": "square", "side": s}:\n        print("square")`, out: `2.0 None\nfloat | None\na 3\n42\nwoof\nmoving to (3, 4)\nsaying: hello world\nbye\ncomment\nunknown\ncircle of radius 2` },
  lineByLine: [["list[float] and float | None", "Modern generic and union syntax."], ["__annotations__[\"return\"]", "The hint is stored but not enforced."], ["TypeVar T", "The returned type matches the item type of the list."], ["Callable[[int], int]", "A function from int to int."], ["class Speaker(Protocol)", "Any class with a speak() method matches, no inheritance needed."], ["case [\"move\", x, y]", "Matches a 3-item list starting with move and captures x and y."], ["case [\"quit\"] | [\"exit\"]", "OR pattern."], ["case [name, *_] if ...", "A guard adds a condition."], ["case {\"type\": \"circle\", \"r\": r}", "A mapping pattern."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Matching <code>\"say hello world\"</code>:", headers: ["Pattern", "Result"], rows: [["[\"move\", x, y]", "no: first item is 'say'"], ["[\"say\", *words]", "yes: words = ['hello', 'world']"], ["body runs", "'saying: hello world'"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Hints make a small function self-documenting, and the checker would flag a wrong call before it runs.", code: `def discount(price: float, percent: float = 10) -> float:\n    return round(price * (100 - percent) / 100, 2)\n\nprint(discount(999.99), discount(200, 25))\n# discount("200")  <- a type checker rejects this line without running it`, out: `899.99 150.0` },
  mistakes: ["Believing hints are enforced at run time.", "Using <code>list</code> as a hint when you mean <code>list[int]</code>.", "In match, writing a variable name expecting a comparison (it captures).", "Forgetting the wildcard case.", "Running match code on Python older than 3.10."],
  practices: ["Hint public functions and data models first.", "Run <code>mypy</code> or <code>pyright</code> in CI.", "Use <code>X | None</code> instead of <code>Optional[X]</code> on 3.10+.", "Keep patterns simple; use if/elif for simple value tests."],
  interview: ["Are type hints enforced?", "What is Optional?", "How is match different from a switch?", "What is a Protocol?"],
  practiceQs: ["Annotate a function taking a dict of marks and returning the best name.", "Write a match that handles [\"add\", a, b] and [\"neg\", a].", "What is the difference between int | None and Optional[int]?", "Why does case x: capture instead of compare?"],
  revision: ["Hints: variables, parameters, returns; list[int], X | None.", "Checked by mypy/pyright, ignored by Python.", "match: literal, capture, sequence, mapping, class, guard, _.", "Python 3.10+ for match."],
  hinglish: "Type hints sirf padhne walon aur mypy jaise tools ke liye hote hain, Python unhe run time par check nahi karta. match/case structure ko pattern se milakar values nikalta hai (Python 3.10+). case _ sab ke liye default hai.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("Does Python enforce type hints at run time?", ["Yes always", "No, tools like mypy check them", "Only for ints", "Only in functions"], 1, "They are metadata."),
    mcq("Which is the modern spelling of Optional[int]?", ["int or None", "int | None", "None[int]", "Maybe[int]"], 1, "The | union syntax needs Python 3.10+."),
    mcq("What does case [\"move\", x, y] do?", ["Compares to variables x and y", "Matches a three-item sequence and captures x and y", "Declares x and y", "Raises an error"], 1, "Names in patterns capture values."),
    mcq("Which case matches anything?", ["case any:", "case _:", "case *:", "case else:"], 1, "The wildcard underscore."),
    mcq("A Protocol class describes:", ["A base class everyone must inherit", "The methods an object must have, without inheritance", "A database", "A decorator"], 1, "Structural typing.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Command interpreter", "Standard input holds one command per line. Use match/case: move X Y prints Moving to (X, Y); say followed by words prints Saying: and the words; quit prints Bye; anything else prints Unknown command.", "import sys\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n    command = line.split()\n    # handle the command with match/case\n", `import sys\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n    command = line.split()\n    match command:\n        case ["move", x, y]:\n            print(f"Moving to ({x}, {y})")\n        case ["say", *words]:\n            print("Saying: " + " ".join(words))\n        case ["quit"]:\n            print("Bye")\n        case _:\n            print("Unknown command")`, [["move 3 4\nsay hello world\nquit", "Moving to (3, 4)\nSaying: hello world\nBye"], ["jump", "Unknown command"]], [["move 3 4\nsay hello world\nquit", "Moving to (3, 4)\nSaying: hello world\nBye"], ["jump", "Unknown command"], ["move 1\nmove 1 2 3", "Unknown command\nUnknown command"], ["say a", "Saying: a"]])],
});

