lessons.push({
  title: "datetime, math, random and logging",
  minutes: 50,
  learn: ["Work with dates, times and differences correctly", "Format and parse dates", "Use math and random sensibly", "Replace print debugging with the logging module"],
  why: "Dates and times cause more bugs than almost any other topic. Logging is how real applications report what they are doing without cluttering the output.",
  realWorld: "Computing the days until a deadline, generating an OTP, writing audit logs for a server.",
  simple: "<code>datetime</code> represents dates and times and lets you add, subtract and format them. <code>logging</code> is a configurable replacement for <code>print</code> with levels: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL.",
  detail: `<h3>datetime essentials</h3>
${table(["Need", "Code"], [["today's date / now", "<code>date.today()</code>, <code>datetime.now()</code>"], ["make a date", "<code>date(2026, 11, 2)</code>"], ["difference", "<code>(d2 - d1).days</code> (a <code>timedelta</code>)"], ["add time", "<code>d + timedelta(days=30)</code>"], ["format", "<code>d.strftime(\"%d %b %Y\")</code>"], ["parse text", "<code>datetime.strptime(\"2026-11-02\", \"%Y-%m-%d\")</code>"], ["ISO text", "<code>d.isoformat()</code>, <code>date.fromisoformat(\"2026-11-02\")</code>"], ["weekday", "<code>d.weekday()</code> (Monday is 0), <code>d.strftime(\"%A\")</code>"], ["time zones", "<code>datetime.now(timezone.utc)</code>, <code>zoneinfo.ZoneInfo(\"Asia/Kolkata\")</code>"]])}
<p>Naive datetimes (no time zone) are ambiguous. Store and compare times in UTC and convert for display. Common codes: <code>%Y</code> year, <code>%m</code> month, <code>%d</code> day, <code>%H:%M:%S</code>, <code>%B</code> month name.</p>
<h3>math</h3>
<p><code>sqrt</code>, <code>ceil</code>, <code>floor</code>, <code>gcd</code>, <code>lcm</code>, <code>comb</code>, <code>perm</code>, <code>factorial</code>, <code>prod</code>, <code>isclose</code>, <code>log</code>, <code>pi</code>, <code>inf</code>. Use <code>math.isclose(a, b)</code> to compare floats.</p>
<h3>random</h3>
<p><code>random()</code>, <code>randint(a, b)</code> (inclusive), <code>choice</code>, <code>sample</code>, <code>shuffle</code>, <code>seed</code>. These are <strong>not secure</strong>: for passwords, tokens and OTPs use the <code>secrets</code> module (<code>secrets.randbelow</code>, <code>secrets.token_hex</code>).</p>
<h3>logging</h3>
<pre style="background:rgba(127,127,127,.14);padding:12px 14px;border-radius:8px"><code>import logging\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format="%(levelname)s %(name)s: %(message)s")\nlog = logging.getLogger(__name__)\nlog.info("loaded %d rows", 120)\nlog.error("failed", exc_info=True)</code></pre>
<p>Libraries log; applications configure (level, format, file). Use <code>%s</code>-style arguments rather than f-strings so formatting happens only when the message is emitted. <code>log.exception("...")</code> inside an <code>except</code> logs the traceback. Never log passwords or personal data.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `from datetime import date, datetime, timedelta\n(date(2026, 12, 25) - date(2026, 11, 2)).days\nd.strftime("%d %b %Y")\nimport logging\nlog = logging.getLogger(__name__)`, note: "Use UTC for storage." },
  diagram: chain("Logging levels, low to high", "Left to right: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL; a configured level shows that level and everything to its right.",
    [{ k: "proc", t: "DEBUG" }, { k: "proc", t: "INFO" }, { k: "proc", t: "WARNING" }, { k: "proc", t: "ERROR" }, { k: "term", t: "CRITICAL" }], { w: 112, gap: 22, hgt: 44 }),
  example: { code: `from datetime import date, datetime, timedelta\nimport math, random, logging, io\n\nd1, d2 = date(2026, 11, 2), date(2026, 12, 25)\nprint((d2 - d1).days, d1.strftime("%A, %d %B %Y"), d1 + timedelta(days=30))\nt = datetime.strptime("2026-11-02 18:30", "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M")\nprint(t.hour, t.isoformat(), t + timedelta(hours=7))\nprint(date.fromisoformat("2026-02-28") + timedelta(days=1))\n\nprint(math.ceil(2.1), math.floor(-2.1), math.comb(5, 2), math.isclose(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3))\nrandom.seed(3)\nrolls = [random.randint(1, 6) for _ in range(3)]\nprint(len(rolls), all(1 <= r <= 6 for r in rolls))\n\nstream = io.StringIO()\nlogging.basicConfig(stream=stream, level=logging.INFO, format="%(levelname)s:%(name)s:%(message)s", force=True)\nlog = logging.getLogger("shop")\nlog.debug("hidden")\nlog.info("loaded %d rows", 120)\nlog.warning("slow response")\ntry:\n    1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    log.error("calculation failed")\nprint(stream.getvalue().strip())`, out: `53 Monday, 02 November 2026 2026-12-02\n18 2026-11-02T18:30:00 2026-11-03 01:30:00\n2026-03-01\n3 -3 10 True\n3 True\nINFO:shop:loaded 120 rows\nWARNING:shop:slow response\nERROR:shop:calculation failed` },
  lineByLine: [["(d2 - d1).days", "Subtracting dates gives a timedelta."], ["strftime(\"%A, %d %B %Y\")", "Formats with weekday and month names."], ["strptime(..., \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\")", "Parses text into a datetime according to the format."], ["date(2026, 2, 28) + timedelta(days=1)", "2026 is not a leap year, so the next day is 1 March."], ["math.isclose(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3)", "The safe way to compare floats."], ["basicConfig(level=INFO)", "DEBUG messages are suppressed; the rest are shown."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Which messages appear at <code>level=INFO</code>?", headers: ["Call", "Level", "Shown?"], rows: [["log.debug(\"hidden\")", "DEBUG", "no (below INFO)"], ["log.info(...)", "INFO", "yes"], ["log.warning(...)", "WARNING", "yes"], ["log.error(...)", "ERROR", "yes"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Secure random values for security uses, and a deadline check.", code: `import secrets\nfrom datetime import date\notp = f"{secrets.randbelow(10**6):06d}"\nprint(len(otp), otp.isdigit())\ndeadline = date(2026, 11, 30)\ntoday = date(2026, 11, 2)\nprint(f"{(deadline - today).days} days left")`, out: `6 True\n28 days left` },
  mistakes: ["Using <code>random</code> for passwords or OTPs.", "Naive datetimes across time zones.", "Forgetting that <code>randint</code> includes both ends.", "Comparing floats with ==.", "Logging with f-strings and with sensitive data."],
  practices: ["Store UTC, display local.", "Use <code>pathlib</code> and <code>logging</code> instead of print and string paths in real programs.", "Seed random in tests for repeatable results.", "Use <code>getLogger(__name__)</code> per module."],
  interview: ["Difference between date, datetime and timedelta?", "Why not use random for tokens?", "What are logging levels?", "How do you compare two floats?"],
  practiceQs: ["How many days between 1 Jan 2026 and 1 Jan 2027?", "Format a date as 02/11/2026.", "Which level would you use for a failed payment?", "Why use %s style in log calls?"],
  revision: ["date/datetime/timedelta; strftime/strptime.", "UTC for storage.", "math.isclose; secrets for security.", "Logging levels and getLogger."],
  hinglish: "Do dates ka antar timedelta deta hai (.days). strftime date ko text banata hai aur strptime text ko date. Password aur OTP ke liye random nahi secrets use karo. print ki jagah logging use karo, jisme DEBUG se CRITICAL tak levels hote hain.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What does (date(2026, 3, 1) - date(2026, 2, 1)).days return?", ["28", "29", "30", "31"], 0, "February 2026 has 28 days.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("Which module should generate a login OTP?", ["random", "secrets", "math", "time"], 1, "secrets uses a cryptographically secure source."),
    mcq("Which logging level is higher than WARNING?", ["INFO", "DEBUG", "ERROR", "NOTSET"], 2, "DEBUG < INFO < WARNING < ERROR < CRITICAL."),
    mcq("What does strptime do?", ["Formats a date as text", "Parses text into a datetime", "Adds days", "Prints the time zone"], 1, "p = parse."),
    mcq("How should floats be compared?", ["==", "math.isclose", "is", "in"], 1, "isclose tolerates tiny representation errors.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Day of the week and countdown", "Read two dates in ISO format (YYYY-MM-DD) on separate lines. Print the weekday name of the first date (for example Monday), and on the next line the number of days from the first date to the second.", "from datetime import date\nstart = date.fromisoformat(input())\nend = date.fromisoformat(input())\n# print the weekday name of start, then the number of days to end\n", `from datetime import date\nstart = date.fromisoformat(input())\nend = date.fromisoformat(input())\nprint(start.strftime("%A"))\nprint((end - start).days)`, [["2026-11-02\n2026-12-25", "Monday\n53"], ["2026-01-01\n2027-01-01", "Thursday\n365"]], [["2026-11-02\n2026-12-25", "Monday\n53"], ["2026-01-01\n2027-01-01", "Thursday\n365"], ["2024-02-28\n2024-03-01", "Wednesday\n2"], ["2026-11-02\n2026-11-02", "Monday\n0"], ["2026-12-25\n2026-11-02", "Friday\n-53"]])],
});

