lessons.push({
  title: "Command-line Arguments and Environment Variables",
  minutes: 40,
  learn: ["Read arguments with sys.argv and argparse", "Build a small command-line tool with options and help", "Read configuration from environment variables safely", "Structure a script with main()"],
  why: "Scripts and tools are run from a terminal with options (<code>--input data.csv --verbose</code>). Configuration and secrets belong in the environment, never in the code.",
  realWorld: "A report script: <code>python report.py --month 11 --format csv</code>. A server reads <code>DATABASE_URL</code> and <code>SECRET_KEY</code> from environment variables.",
  simple: "<code>sys.argv</code> is the raw list of words typed after <code>python</code>. <code>argparse</code> turns them into named, converted, validated values and generates <code>--help</code>. <code>os.environ</code> holds environment variables.",
  detail: `<h3>sys.argv</h3>
<p><code>python tool.py a b</code> gives <code>sys.argv == ["tool.py", "a", "b"]</code>. Everything is a string; counting and converting is on you. Fine for tiny scripts.</p>
<h3>argparse</h3>
<pre style="background:rgba(127,127,127,.14);padding:12px 14px;border-radius:8px"><code>import argparse\nparser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="Greet people")\nparser.add_argument("name")                      # positional\nparser.add_argument("--times", type=int, default=1)\nparser.add_argument("--shout", action="store_true")\nargs = parser.parse_args()                       # or parse_args(list_for_tests)\nprint(args.name, args.times, args.shout)</code></pre>
<p>It provides types, defaults, choices, required options, subcommands and automatic <code>-h/--help</code> and error messages. Passing a list to <code>parse_args(["Asha", "--times", "2"])</code> makes the parser easy to test.</p>
<h3>Environment variables</h3>
<ul><li><code>os.environ["HOME"]</code> (KeyError if missing); <code>os.environ.get("MODE", "dev")</code> or <code>os.getenv</code> with a default.</li><li>Values are always strings: convert numbers and booleans yourself.</li><li>Use them for deployment-specific settings and <strong>secrets</strong> (API keys, database passwords). Never hard-code or commit secrets. During development a <code>.env</code> file (read by <code>python-dotenv</code>) kept out of Git is common.</li></ul>
<h3>Script structure</h3>
<p>Put the work in <code>main()</code> and call it under <code>if __name__ == "__main__":</code>. Return a status code with <code>sys.exit(0)</code> for success and non-zero for failure so other programs can react.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `import argparse, os, sys\ndef main(argv=None):\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    parser.add_argument("--n", type=int, default=3)\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n    mode = os.environ.get("APP_MODE", "dev")\n    return 0\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    sys.exit(main())`, note: "Passing argv makes main() testable." },
  diagram: chain("From the terminal to your code", "Left to right: the command typed in the terminal, sys.argv holds the words, argparse converts them to typed values, the program uses them.",
    [{ k: "io", t: "python tool.py\nAsha --times 2" }, { k: "proc", t: "sys.argv\n(list of strings)" }, { k: "proc", t: "argparse\n(types, defaults)" }, { k: "term", t: "args.name\nargs.times" }], { w: 150, gap: 28, hgt: 62 }),
  example: { code: `import argparse, os, shlex, sys\n\ndef main(argv=None):\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="Greet people")\n    parser.add_argument("name")\n    parser.add_argument("--times", type=int, default=1)\n    parser.add_argument("--shout", action="store_true")\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n    text = f"Hello, {args.name}!"\n    for _ in range(args.times):\n        print(text.upper() if args.shout else text)\n    return 0\n\nmain(["Asha"])\nmain(shlex.split("Ravi --times 2 --shout"))\ntry:\n    main(["Zoya", "--times", "many"])\nexcept SystemExit as stop:\n    print("exit code", stop.code)\n\nos.environ["APP_MODE"] = "test"\nprint(os.environ.get("APP_MODE", "dev"), os.environ.get("NOT_SET", "default"))\nprint(len(sys.argv) >= 1)`, out: `Hello, Asha!\nHELLO, RAVI!\nHELLO, RAVI!\nexit code 2\ntest default\nTrue` },
  lineByLine: [["parser.add_argument(\"name\")", "A required positional argument."], ["--times, type=int, default=1", "An optional option converted to int."], ["action=\"store_true\"", "A flag that is False unless given."], ["main(argv)", "Passing a list means the same code runs from a test."], ["SystemExit with code 2", "argparse prints a usage error and exits with status 2 on invalid input."], ["os.environ.get(\"NOT_SET\", \"default\")", "A safe default instead of a KeyError."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Parsing <code>Ravi --times 2 --shout</code>:", headers: ["Word", "Becomes"], rows: [["Ravi", "args.name = \"Ravi\""], ["--times 2", "args.times = 2 (int)"], ["--shout", "args.shout = True"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "A configuration object built from the environment with defaults and conversions.", code: `import os\nos.environ["PORT"] = "8080"\nos.environ["DEBUG"] = "true"\nconfig = {\n    "port": int(os.environ.get("PORT", "5000")),\n    "debug": os.environ.get("DEBUG", "false").lower() in ("1", "true", "yes"),\n    "db": os.environ.get("DATABASE_URL", "sqlite:///dev.db"),\n}\nprint(config)`, out: `{'port': 8080, 'debug': True, 'db': 'sqlite:///dev.db'}` },
  mistakes: ["Hard-coding secrets in source files.", "Forgetting that arguments and variables are strings.", "Using <code>os.environ[\"X\"]</code> without handling KeyError.", "Parsing <code>sys.argv</code> by hand for anything more than one option.", "Committing a <code>.env</code> file."],
  practices: ["Use argparse for anything with options.", "Give every setting a safe default.", "Keep secrets in the environment or a secrets manager.", "Write <code>main()</code> and guard it."],
  interview: ["What is sys.argv?", "Why use environment variables for configuration?", "Why pass argv to main()?", "How does argparse report errors?"],
  practiceQs: ["Add a --verbose flag to a script.", "Read an optional environment variable as an integer.", "What does sys.exit(1) mean to the shell?", "Why should secrets not be in the repository?"],
  revision: ["sys.argv: list of strings.", "argparse: positional, options, types, flags, help.", "os.environ.get with defaults; strings only.", "main() with a guard; exit codes."],
  hinglish: "sys.argv terminal mein likhe shabdon ki list hoti hai. argparse unhe naam, type aur default ke saath deta hai aur --help bhi banata hai. Password aur API key code mein nahi, environment variable mein rakho.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What is sys.argv[0]?", ["The first argument", "The script name", "The Python version", "The user name"], 1, "Arguments start at index 1."),
    mcq("What type are values in os.environ?", ["str", "int", "bool", "Any"], 0, "Always strings."),
    mcq("Which module builds a --help screen automatically?", ["sys", "argparse", "os", "logging"], 1, "argparse generates usage and help."),
    mcq("What does action=\"store_true\" create?", ["A required string", "A flag that is True when given", "A list", "A file"], 1, "Flags default to False."),
    mcq("Where should an API key be kept?", ["In the source code", "In an environment variable or secrets manager", "In a public repository", "In the README"], 1, "Never commit secrets.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Greeting arguments", "Read one line containing command-line style arguments such as: Asha --times 2. Use argparse (parse the line with shlex.split) with a positional name and an optional --times integer (default 1). Print Hello, <name>! that many times.", "import argparse, shlex\nline = input()\n# parse the arguments and print the greeting\n", `import argparse, shlex\nline = input()\nparser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\nparser.add_argument("name")\nparser.add_argument("--times", type=int, default=1)\nargs = parser.parse_args(shlex.split(line))\nfor _ in range(args.times):\n    print(f"Hello, {args.name}!")`, [["Asha --times 2", "Hello, Asha!\nHello, Asha!"], ["Ravi", "Hello, Ravi!"]], [["Asha --times 2", "Hello, Asha!\nHello, Asha!"], ["Ravi", "Hello, Ravi!"], ["--times 3 Zoya", "Hello, Zoya!\nHello, Zoya!\nHello, Zoya!"], ["Kiran --times 1", "Hello, Kiran!"]])],
});

